          "derivation": "A textbook Persian → Arabic → Persian round trip. The Iranian etymon (MP zūr) went into Arabic as zūr (node #4), was fitted there with Arabic Form-II morphology, and came back into New Persian after the conquest as two ordinary Arabic loanwords: تزویر tazvir 'forgery, dissimulation, hypocrisy' (Ar. verbal noun tafʿīl of zawwara) and مزوّر mozavvar 'deceitful, hypocritical; forged' (Ar. participle). Neither could have been formed inside Persian: the internal vowel pattern (ta-C₁C₂īC₃, mu-C₁aC₂C₂aC₃) is purely Arabic templatic morphology, which is what makes the return trip visible. Mo'in accordingly tags them [ع.] (Arabic) while using them throughout as normal Persian words for 'trickery, deceit'. So the words are Persian in use, Arabic in shape, and Iranian in root — exactly the three-step Nourai draws.",
