          "derivation": "All three descend from Latin fallere/falsus, so the arrow Latin → English is right; only the link from Latin up to the root is doubtful (node #7). (a) false: Latin falsus was borrowed into Old English very early as fals 'fraud, falsehood' (a Christian-Latin loan), then re-supplied in the 13th c. by Old French fals, faus (mod. faux) → Middle English fals → false; the sense 'intentionally untrue, lying' is the late Old English one. (b) fail: Latin fallere was reshaped in Vulgar Latin as a fourth-conjugation *fallīre 'to be lacking, come short', giving Old French falir/faillir → Middle English failen (c. 1200); the noun fail is from Old French faille. (c) failure: not a Latin word at all but an English refashioning — Anglo-French failer, the infinitive used as a noun (Middle English failer 'default', c. 1400), respelt -ure from the 1660s on the model of pleasure, seizure. The same verb also gave English fault (< OF faute < VL *fallita), default, fallacy, fallible and (via Italian) falsetto. Nourai's AHD:470,473 are the main-dictionary pages of the 1975 New College edition (fail, false), which is not the volume held locally.",
